vious text gave the impression that the members of the Inaba family had been living in Kakuzawa City since birth. Readers might have thought that their aloof and distant personalities were formed due to growing up in Kakuzawa City, and that they placed a great deal of importance on this cause-and-effect relationship. Additionally, before Woodstock's correction, everyone probably believed that Akihiko and Akihiko never left Kakuzawa City. [1937] However, this is a misunderstanding. [1938] Just now - when we finished recording Akihiko's confession and returned to the third-floor room, Woodstock corrected my misconception. [1939] \"You don't know anything, do you?\" [1940] Woodstock was surprised (half in shock) and then explained to me. [1941] So, please rest assured, readers. You received this information later than Mutoh, but it was synchronous with me. The information was released a bit late, but you don't have to worry about deviating in your reasoning. [1942] The most important thing is that the case has just entered the middle stage. [1943] To get back to the point, I will now briefly summarize the history of the Oyama family and the changes in Kurenaga Castle based on the information from the Mutoh Detective Agency (apologies - I can't believe I said it - the summary will be in the form of an outline compiled by myself). [1944] After World War II, the original Oyama family mansion, Kurenaga Castle, became a humiliating villa. Due to transportation difficulties, the Oyama family lived in another residence in Katsushika for a period of time. [1945] Twenty-five years ago, Oyama Motoharu, who had resigned as president, moved to Kurenaga Castle with his wife, Hinata. They seemed to be planning to live a secluded life together. [1946] Tetsuzo Murakami's son (who had three sons named Ito, Ogata, and Kubo) had already taken up important positions and were scattered across Tokyo, Osaka, and various regional cities. So, they hired Yashiro as a housekeeper to take care of the two elderly people. [1947] For a while, the only residents of the castle were Tetsuzo Murakami, Fujiko, and Yashiro. [1948] Kurokawa Castle saw new faces in Showa 49. The dignified Hiruma family entrusted their five-year-old twins, Wakaba and Kanae, to their grandparents. It was said that Hiruma's wife didn't want to be separated from her daughter, but she eventually agreed with Hiruma. [1949] However, the pain of separation caused Hiruma's wife to pass away six months later. Yashiro then took on the responsibility of raising the twins. [1950] For the next fifteen years, Kurokawa Castle remained largely unchanged. Perhaps due to the painful memories, Hiruma never approached Wakaba and Kanae, Tetsuzo Murakami's sons were busy with their work and never visited, and Tetsuzo Murakami and his wife rarely went out. [1951] In a sense, this may have been the most peaceful period in the history of Inabu Castle. [1952] The tension at Inabu Castle was broken two years after Hinata-ko's death. Motsu was deeply affected and probably felt the loneliness of being alone. He called his long-estranged sons back to Inabu Castle. [1953] With the increase in population, Inabu Castle hired new chefs, maids, and farmhands. The current residents of the castle were brought together five months ago when Yamanaka-san took in Miyuki against the protests of Inabu. [1954] One month ago, Motsu passed away. His health had been declining since Hinata-ko's death, and he looked like a skeleton before he died. [1955] Two days ago...\n[1956] What happened next is already known to the readers. [1957] \"Motoshige's death was like removing a golden ring,\" said Mukai, devouring his lunch, which had been delayed by three hours. \"He died a month ago, didn't he?\" [1958] Just a month ago, the commander of the Ito Group, Motoshige, passed away. Meanwhile, Yodo, Uma, and Kouzu were killed, seemingly following him. The series of murders made it seem like Motoshige's death had occurred a long time ago, but in reality, the forty-nine-day memorial service had not yet ended. [1959] \"Just as the decline of the Ashikaga shogunate marked the beginning of the Sengoku period, it seems that the long-standing equilibrium has been disrupted by Motoshige's death.\" [1960] \"So fragile?\" [1961] \"Well, they had been checking each other, so that's a fact, isn't it?\" [1962] If Mut\u014dmochi had forced his sons to gather under his feet through some kind of power (maybe inheritance), it would not be surprising if some negative effects had arisen. [1963] \"This woman named 'Uzuki' doesn't seem to have come to Kuraj\u014d Castle. It says she is Mut\u014dmochi's daughter.\" [1964] Woodstock recorded the Mikage family tree, where the name of a woman unknown to others was written. She was Mut\u014dmochi's youngest daughter, and instead of whether she was alive or dead, a question mark was written. [1965] \"She's missing.\" [1966] \"Missing?\" [1967] \"Yeah, I heard she ran away with a man thirty years ago and hasn't been heard from since. I'm having someone investigate, but I haven't gotten any clear answers yet.\" [1968] \"Running away with a man? She's D\u014dsh\u014d's only daughter, isn't she?\" I couldn't help but feel uneasy. [1969] \"It's selfishness!,\" I said. [1970] \"That's right, Kusunoki never got what he wanted, but his cousin Kujaku chose true love. Of course, I heard that D\u014dsh\u014d was furious and cut off all ties with Kujaku, and Kusunoki would have been afraid of that too.\" [1971] When I think of Fujiko, I don't feel like he deserves any sympathy. [1972] \"You can run around on your own emotional track, but don't forget the main point.\" [1973] \"Are you talking about the killer?\" [1974] \"Of course.\u201d He confirmed it nonchalantly. [1975] \"I don't understand. You've shattered everything you said before.\" [1976] \"You make me sound like a bad guy.\" [1977] \"That's not it. The key is, what are your thoughts? Are you thinking of some divine intervention again?\" [1978] \"Are you asking me?\" [1979] Mochizuki placed the salad plate in front of himself. He seemed to have thought of a new \"ingredient.\" [1980] \"I'm thinking of a new suspect.\" [1981] \"A new suspect?\" [1982] \"Yes. It's Hiruko\u2026 or her descendants.\" [1983] \"\u2026That's terrifying.\" [1984] \"It's not impossible.\" Woodmore said with a smile. [1985] \"It could be a mystery novel\u2026 Do you mean the killer is from outside?\" [1986] \"But that would disrupt the system we've established so far. Along with this possibility comes a great deal of danger.\" [1987] \"So, everything has to start over again? Are you thinking of a scenario where Kusaka kills everyone in the Inagaki family and then reveals her identity?\" [1988] \"That's a very classical plot. However, in the case of Arai's murder, a stranger would not be able to wander around the house in broad daylight.\" [1989] \"So, the suspects also include you and me, as well as Detective Tsujimura and other members of the police, right?\" [1990] \"That's right.\" [1991] Wakuzuka nodded as if waiting for me to say that. [1992] \"To a third party who means well, you and I could very well be Kurahashi's children.\" [1993] \"Hah\u2026\" I chuckled, \"That would make things even more confusing.\" [1994] \"I was just giving an example.\" [1995] Wakuzuka finished his tomato juice. It was a recent trial product, a one-liter box, and he had managed to drink the entire thing. [1996] \"Alright, let's go see Yoshino. I have a few things to ask him.\" [1997] As the door opened, a somber melody flowed out from the house. [1998] The interplay of high and low tones seemed to be the product of transferring the world's sorrows into the realm of ideas and concretizing them. The sweet yet slightly bitter accompaniment of the viola released a suppressed radiance. [1999] The lively rhythm of the piano was merely a \"false flower\" here. The low-pitched strumming evoked a longing reaching towards a distant heaven. [2000] Yoshino was alone in the house, buried in the sofa. He closed his eyes and occasionally moved with the music, fully immersed in the delicate flow of sound. [2001] The record quietly spun, and the needle emitted a faint noise. [2002] Despite the lack of vocals, this piano quintet was like a requiem, melancholy yet clear. Ah, was this perhaps a pure music requiem mass? [2003] In the corner of the room on the right hung a monochrome painting. It was probably a woodcut. A half-naked man resembling Jesus was nailed to the cross. This seemed to be a religious painting depicting the crucifixion of Christ, with an overall sense of decay, yet somehow harmoniously blended with the music, as if responding to each other. [2004] \"The melody is very sad.\" [2005] Mr. Mochizuki spoke, and Mr. Ogawa stood up, as if he had just noticed someone had come in. He hastily straightened himself, as if he were embarrassed to be caught in a compromising position. [2006] \"I'm sorry, I just came in without knocking. Although I did knock on the door.\" [2007] \"No, no, Mutsuken-san, please come in.\" [2008] Kaname looked a little tired. After all, it had only been half a day since his father's death. [2009] \"What is this piece?\" Mutsuken sat down opposite Kaname and asked immediately. [2010] In terms of classical music, Mutsuken's knowledge should be deeper than mine. It must be a very obscure piece that even he doesn't know. [2011] \"This piece?\" Kumagai asked in surprise. \"It's the third movement of Medtner's piano quintet.\" [2012] \"Medtner? Is he Russian?\" [2013] Woodside seemed just as unfamiliar with the composer's name. His lack of knowledge must mean that he is a figure without a mention in any music dictionary. [2014] \"Yes, his full name is Mikhail Ivanovich Medtner. He was a Russian composer in the early 20th century. It's not surprising you don't know him. He was erased from history.\" [2015] Kumagai's expression was shrouded in shadow. [2016] \"Erased? That's quite unusual,\" Mu Jin said with a dramatic gesture. [2017] \"Yes, it happens often. Medvedev was the leader of the Imperial Court Orchestra and the music teacher of the Crown Prince. Because of his position,\u2026\"\n[2018] \"Was it the Russian Revolution?\" [2019] At this moment, the first violin began to play a Russian-style overstatement, as if reacting sensitively to Mu Jin's words. [2020] \"Yes, Medvedev fled and survived, but his compositions were destroyed by the Bolsheviks. He reportedly had a few symphonies and operas.\" [2021] \"I haven't heard of that.\" [2022] \"It is said that, due to the influence of economic liberalization reforms, Russia has seen a reevaluation trend. However, he was a figure before the revolution, so it is unclear how reliable this information is.\" [2023] \"But didn't Prokofiev and Rachmaninoff both undergo reevaluation?\" [2024] Prokofiev, who returned to his homeland, need not be mentioned, and Soviet pianists often mention the concerto composed by Rachmaninoff, who settled in America. [2025] \"Medvedev is not just a musician, it is said that he has also deeply participated in political activities. There were even rumors that he plotted some political conspiracies with Rasputin. Although he has never appeared on the historical stage.\" [2026] \"Why do you have his works here?\" Mukai pointed to the record. [2027] \"This is a so-called private record, and only the Omotos have it.\" [2028] \"What's going on?\" [2029] \"Did you know that Sergei Prokofiev, who fled during the revolution, once lived in Japan?\" [2030] \"I know.\" [2031] During his exile, Prokofiev was almost unknown in Japan, so he didn't attract much attention. Nevertheless, his solo concert at the Imperial Theater still amazed a group of enthusiasts at the time. [2032] \"Medvedev also fled to Japan. At that time, Prokofiev stayed in Tokyo, and Medvedev was said to have lived in our Inagaki family. This was before I was born.\" [2033] \"Here?\" [2034] Mukai seemed truly surprised. It seems that this story did not fall into his intelligence network. [2035] \"As I mentioned earlier, Medvedev was also a political prisoner, so he was afraid of the Red Terror and hid in the northern mountains. No one knew about it, not even the Inagaki family, except for a few people.\" [2036] \"Is he staying in this house?\" [2037] \"Yes, on the third floor, across from your room, Murakami-san.\" [2038] This was during the pre-war period when the Kuj\u014d residence was still used as the main house. [2039] \"Is this the song he composed while staying here?\" [2040] \"He dedicated this piece to my grandfather. The song is called 'Imakagami.' This record was made thirty years ago by a record company. At the time, Medvedev's name was omitted, and it was claimed to be composed by my grandfather.\" [2041] Looking up, I saw that the record sleeve indeed read \"Composed by Tomomichi Imai.\" [2042] \"However, this song is quite dark. It seems to require a high level of technique from the cello soloist.\" [2043] \"Isn't that true for an artist who has lost his country?\" [2044] Kanai sighed and lowered his eyes again. The mournful wails of the first and second violins merged together. [2045] For a moment, we stopped talking and listened to this requiem arranged in a quintet. [2046] Was Medvedev in Japan, reminiscing about St. Petersburg or mourning the Romanov dynasty that fell into tragedy? [2047] \"This piece became his final work,\" Kanayama suddenly said. \"One reason he didn't leave any other works after his escape was that he could only create this one piece. Medvedev passed away six months after arriving in Kurosawa City.\" [2048] \"Did he die of disappointment?\" [2049] \"No\u2026\" Kanayama sadly shook his head. \"He was over fifty at the time, so his body was already weak\u2026 However, he drowned, and his body was found in the pond behind the house.\" [2050] \"Drowned\u2026 Was he murdered?\" [2051] Mutsu's words were cautious, but he was clearly shocked.